The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (ide_dev)
    dev_dbg(&ide_dev->dev, "Bus master activity detection (BM-IDE) erratum enabled\n");
if (isa_dev)
    dev_dbg(&isa_dev->dev, "Type-F DMA livelock erratum (C3 disabled)\n");
Reason:
These statements are executed after pci_dev_put() has been called for ide_dev and isa_dev, so they dereference device pointers that may have been freed, causing a use-after-free. Additionally, they are executed unconditionally even when the corresponding errata flags are not set, printing misleading debug messages.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (ide_dev) {
    errata.piix4.bmisx = pci_resource_start(ide_dev, 4);
    if (errata.piix4.bmisx)
        dev_dbg(&ide_dev->dev, "Bus master activity detection (BM-IDE) erratum enabled\n");
    pci_dev_put(ide_dev);
}
if (isa_dev) {
    pci_read_config_byte(isa_dev, 0x76, &value1);
    pci_read_config_byte(isa_dev, 0x77, &value2);
    if ((value1 & 0x80) || (value2 & 0x80)) {
        errata.piix4.fdma = 1;
        dev_dbg(&isa_dev->dev, "Type-F DMA livelock erratum (C3 disabled)\n");
    }
    pci_dev_put(isa_dev);
}
Reason:
The dev_dbg calls are moved inside the blocks that set the corresponding errata flags, ensuring they are only printed when the errata is actually enabled, and they are placed before pci_dev_put() to avoid use-after-free. This fixes both the use-after-free and the unconditional printing issues.

